Actually, "fullscreen DOS" should be 50 rows of text.  Even though certain
applications react unpridictably under this display, those applications
weren't properly programmed. 

While the standard 25 rows work, there have been an increase of
applications that simply dump their usage across 30-50 lines (and in some
applications ported from unix, multiple pages.) 

Technically, Nethack should temporarly switch to the 25-line display mode,
and return to the previous mode when finished (if applicable).  The same
applies to any other application that doesn't use the 50 lines to it's
maximum.

(BTW, there are also special VESA text modes, such as 132x43, which are
even higher resolution.  This is used less often, but good applications
shouldn't have a messed display becuase of the additional columns.)


